# Chapter 12 · Case Study III: Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy

> "**Discuss the background of the two Oscar-winning documentaries by Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy**" (==2020==). MCQs asked what *A Girl in the River* was about (**honour killing**, 2019) and what she is famous as (**filmmaker**, 2025).

## 12.1  Who she is

**Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy** was born in ==1978== in **Karachi**. She studied at **Smith College** in the United States and took master's degrees from **Stanford University**. She began as a journalist and turned to documentary film to tell stories of injustice, especially against women and children. She is the **first Pakistani to win an Academy Award (Oscar)** — and she has won **two**.

::: fig timeline | Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy's career.
@layout vertical
1978: born in Karachi
2002: first documentary, Terror's Children, on Afghan refugee children
2010: Emmy for Pakistan: Children of the Taliban
2012: Oscar for Saving Face (acid attacks); Hilal-i-Imtiaz; TIME 100
2016: second Oscar for A Girl in the River: The Price of Forgiveness (honour killing)
2016: honour-killing law passed in October
2022: directs episodes of Marvel's Ms. Marvel
:::

Her company, **SOC Films**, has made more than a dozen films. She also co-directed Pakistan's first animated feature, ***3 Bahadur*** (==2015==), and founded projects for children's education and for women's safety.

## 12.2  The first Oscar — *Saving Face* (2012)

### (1) Background: acid attacks in Pakistan

**Acid attacks** — throwing acid, usually on a woman's face — were a growing crime in the 2000s, especially in southern **Punjab**, where acid used in cotton processing was cheap and easy to buy. Attacks were often revenge for refusing a marriage proposal or sexual advances, or arose from domestic and family disputes. Victims were left blind, disfigured and isolated; perpetrators were rarely punished.

### (2) The film

***Saving Face*** (==2012==), co-directed with the American filmmaker **Daniel Junge**, follows the British-Pakistani plastic surgeon **Dr Mohammad Jawad**, who returned to Pakistan to perform reconstructive surgery on acid-attack survivors. It tells the stories of two women — **Zakia**, attacked by her husband, who seeks justice in court, and **Rukhsana**, attacked by her husband and in-laws, who has to return to live with them because she has nowhere else to go. It also shows the work of activists and the legislators who pushed for a law.

### (3) The award and impact

*Saving Face* won the **Academy Award for Best Documentary (Short Subject)** in ==February 2012== — Pakistan's first Oscar. The film coincided with the passage of the **Acid Control and Acid Crime Prevention Act 2011**, which made acid attacks punishable by **14 years to life imprisonment** and a minimum fine of **one million rupees**. The government awarded her the **Hilal-i-Imtiaz** (==2012==), and *TIME* named her among the world's **100 most influential people**.

## 12.3  The second Oscar — *A Girl in the River* (2016)

### (1) Background: honour killing in Pakistan

Hundreds of women are killed each year in the name of **honour** (Chapter 6). The law allowed the victim's heirs — usually the killer's own family — to **forgive** the killer under the *Qisas* and *Diyat* law, so killers often walked free. A bill to close this loophole, moved by Senator Sughra Imam in ==2014==, had stalled.

### (2) The film

***A Girl in the River: The Price of Forgiveness*** (==2015==) tells the story of **Saba Qaiser**, 19, from a village near **Gujranwala**, Punjab. In ==2014== she married the man she loved, against her family's wishes. Her **father and uncle** shot her in the face, put her in a sack and threw her into a **river**. She survived and dragged herself to the bank. The film follows her recovery and her struggle for justice — and her eventual decision, under heavy pressure from community elders, to **forgive** her father and uncle, who were then released. Her father declared in the film that he had done the right thing.

### (3) The award and impact

The film won the **Academy Award for Best Documentary (Short Subject)** in ==February 2016==. Prime Minister **Nawaz Sharif** hosted a screening at his office and declared, "**There is no honour in honour killing**", promising to change the law. After the murder of **Qandeel Baloch** in July 2016, Parliament passed the **Criminal Law (Amendment) (Offences in the name or pretext of Honour) Act** in ==October 2016==, requiring **life imprisonment** even when the family forgives. The film was also shown on HBO and won an **Emmy**.

::: fig compare | Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy's two Oscar-winning documentaries.
## Saving Face (2012)
- Subject: acid attacks on women
- Story: Dr Mohammad Jawad; survivors Zakia and Rukhsana
- Co-director: Daniel Junge
- Law linked: Acid Control and Acid Crime Prevention Act 2011
- Message: the face as a target of male revenge; justice and repair
## A Girl in the River (2016)
- Subject: honour killing
- Story: Saba Qaiser, 19, shot by father and uncle, survived
- Setting: near Gujranwala, Punjab
- Law linked: honour-killing law, October 2016
- Message: "forgiveness" as the loophole that frees killers
:::

## 12.4  Common themes

1. **Violence against women** as a social, not private, problem.
2. **Impunity** — both films show how families and communities protect perpetrators.
3. **Survivors' agency** — women who resist, seek justice and rebuild their lives.
4. **Film as advocacy** — documentaries timed and used to push for **law reform**.
5. **Pakistan's two faces** — cruelty, but also doctors, lawyers, activists and legislators working for change.

## 12.5  Criticism and debate

- **"Washing dirty linen in public":** some critics in Pakistan accused her of tarnishing the country's image abroad by highlighting its worst problems for Western audiences.
- **Representation:** others argued that such films risk portraying Pakistani women only as victims and Pakistani men only as perpetrators.
- **Defence:** supporters reply that silence protects abusers; her films show survivors' courage and Pakistani reformers, and they helped change laws. She herself has said her aim is to provoke debate at home.

::: balance | Evaluating her contribution
- **Achievements:** two Oscars and international recognition; global attention to acid attacks and honour killings; contribution to the 2011 and 2016 laws; inspiration for Pakistani filmmakers and women.
- **Limits:** laws alone did not end acid attacks or honour killings; Saba Qaiser still had to "forgive" her attackers; critics question the focus on negative images.
-> **Line to take:** Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy shows the power of **media and storytelling** in the fight against GBV — turning hidden crimes into national debates and pushing the state to act — while her films' own stories show that **enforcement and social change** must follow law reform.
:::

## 12.6  The three case studies compared

::: table | The three case studies compared.
@widths 2.6,3.8,3.8,3.8
Aspect | Mukhtaran Mai | Malala Yousafzai | Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy
Role | Survivor and activist | Education activist, Nobel laureate | Filmmaker and advocate
Violence | Gang rape ordered by a panchayat (2002) | Shot by the Taliban (2012) | Films on acid attacks and honour killing
Type of violence | Direct, structural and cultural; "honour" revenge | Political and religious extremism against girls' education | Domestic and honour-based violence
Response | Schools in Meerwala; women's organisation | UN speech, Malala Fund, Nobel Prize (2014) | Two Oscars (2012, 2016); law reform
Legal link | Debate behind the 2006 Act | Article 25A; girls' education policy | Acid Act 2011; honour law 2016
Criticism | State worried about its image | "Western agent"; "I am not Malala" | "Defames Pakistan abroad"
Lesson | Courage against structural injustice | Education as resistance | Media as a tool for change
:::

::: eye | The model structure for 2020
1. Introduction: who she is; first Pakistani Oscar winner. 2. Background of *Saving Face*: acid attacks in Pakistan; the film; Dr Jawad, Zakia, Rukhsana; the Oscar (2012); the Acid Act 2011. 3. Background of *A Girl in the River*: honour killing and the forgiveness loophole; Saba Qaiser; the Oscar (2016); Nawaz Sharif; the October 2016 law. 4. Common themes. 5. Criticism and evaluation. 6. Conclusion. Draw the comparison figure.
:::
